From 2950aa15a171d11757ec4928cb7584a69f6ad9bf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Bedirhan=20Gen=C3=A7aslan?=
 <b2210356065@cs.hacettepe.edu.tr>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 00:59:50 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 24/71] Default to full-access permissions

The `auto` default blocks network access, so npm, pip, and git push all stop
the agent on an approval prompt. Every one of those pauses is a human wait that
also lets the provider's prompt cache expire, and the next request re-bills the
whole prefix uncached. Default the resolved approval policy and built-in
permission profile to full access instead; untrusted projects still start
read-only, an explicit config still wins, and the sandbox itself is unchanged.

Requirements that forbid danger-full-access previously made the config
unloadable once the policy was `never`. That error now only fires when the user
asked for `never` themselves; when full access is merely the default, fall back
to asking for approval rather than refusing to start. On Windows, skip the
sandbox setup prompt when the profile is disabled, since opting out of the
sandbox is exactly what full access means.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.6 <noreply@anthropic.com>
